Yes, you can use the Natural Language Toolkit (NLTK) for PHP to classify each word in a sentence as a noun, verb, adjective, adverb, or other. Here's an example code:
```php
<?php
require_once 'vendor/autoload.php'; // Load the NLTK library
use \NlpTools\Tokenizers\WhitespaceTokenizer;
use \NlpTools\Stemmers\PorterStemmer;
use \NlpTools\Documents\Document;
use \NlpTools\Documents\TokensDocument;
use \NlpTools\FeatureFactories\FeatureFactory;
use \NlpTools\FeatureFactories\DataAsFeatures;
use \NlpTools\Classifiers\MultinomialNBClassifier;
// Define the sentence
$sentence = "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.";
// Tokenize the sentence
$tokenizer = new WhitespaceTokenizer();
$tokens = $tokenizer->tokenize($sentence);
// Stem the tokens
$stemmer = new PorterStemmer();
$stemmed_tokens = array_map(function($token) use ($stemmer) {
    return $stemmer->stem($token);
}, $tokens);
// Create a document from the stemmed tokens
$document = new TokensDocument($stemmed_tokens);
// Define the feature factory
$feature_factory = new FeatureFactory(
    new DataAsFeatures(),
    true // Use binary features
);
// Train the classifier on a pre-labeled dataset
$training_data = array(
    array('noun', array('quick', 'brown', 'fox', 'dog')),
    array('verb', array('jumps')),
    array('adverb', array('over', 'lazy')),
    array('other', array('the'))
);
$classifier = new MultinomialNBClassifier($feature_factory);
$classifier->train($training_data);
// Classify each token in the document
$classifications = array();
foreach ($document->getDocumentData() as $token) {
    $classification = $classifier->classify(array($token));
    $classifications[] = $classification[0];
}
// Print the classifications
foreach ($tokens as $index => $token) {
    echo "$token: {$classifications[$index]}\n";
}
```
This code uses the NLTK library to tokenize the sentence, stem the tokens, and classify each token as a noun, verb, adjective, adverb, or other. The classifier is trained on a pre-labeled dataset, and the feature factory is used to convert the tokens into features that the classifier can use. Finally, the code prints the classifications for each token in the sentence.

