To display a dynamic image gallery on your website using the XML data provided, you can use JavaScript to parse the XML data and create the gallery dynamically. Here's an example code snippet that achieves this:
```
// Get the XML data
var xmlData = "<gallery><image><title>Image 1</title><description>This is the first image</description><url>https://example.com/images/image1.jpg</url></image><image><title>Image 2</title><description>This is the second image</description><url>https://example.com/images/image2.jpg</url></image><image><title>Image 3</title><description>This is the third image</description><url>https://example.com/images/image3.jpg</url></image></gallery>";
// Create a new XML parser
var parser = new DOMParser();
var xmlDoc = parser.parseFromString(xmlData, "text/xml");
// Get the image elements from the XML
var images = xmlDoc.getElementsByTagName("image");
// Create a new gallery element
var gallery = document.createElement("div");
gallery.classList.add("gallery");
// Loop through the images and create image elements
for (var i = 0; i < images.length; i++) {
  var image = images[i];

  // Create a new image element
  var img = document.createElement("img");
  img.src = image.getElementsByTagName("url")[0].childNodes[0].nodeValue;
  img.alt = image.getElementsByTagName("title")[0].childNodes[0].nodeValue;

  // Add the image element to the gallery
  gallery.appendChild(img);
}
// Add the gallery to the page
document.body.appendChild(gallery);
```
This code creates a new `div` element with the class `gallery`, loops through the `image` elements in the XML data, creates a new `img` element for each image, sets the `src` and `alt` attributes of the `img` element based on the data in the XML, and adds the `img` element to the `gallery` element. Finally, the `gallery` element is added to the `body` of the page.
To make the gallery responsive, you can add CSS styles to adjust the size and layout of the `img` elements based on the size of the screen. For example:
```
