To sort the list of pantry items in the most efficient order, we need to calculate the time required to locate any two items in the pantry using the given formula t = ((x2 - x1) + (y2 - y1))/2. We can then sort the items based on increasing time required to locate them. Here's a PHP code that implements this algorithm:
```
$pantry_items = array(
    "Canned soup" => 3,
    "Boxed pasta" => 1,
    "Jarred sauce" => 4,
    "Cereal" => 2
);
function calculate_time($x1, $y1, $x2, $y2) {
    return (($x2 - $x1) + ($y2 - $y1)) / 2;
}
$sorted_items = array();
foreach ($pantry_items as $item1 => $position1) {
    foreach ($pantry_items as $item2 => $position2) {
        if ($item1 != $item2) {
            $time = calculate_time($position1, $position2, $pantry_items[$item1], $pantry_items[$item2]);
            $sorted_items[$item1][$item2] = $time;
        }
    }
}
asort($sorted_items);
foreach ($sorted_items as $item => $times) {
    echo $item . "\n";
}
```
In this code, we first define the pantry items and their positions in the pantry. We then define a function `calculate_time` that takes in the positions of two items and calculates the time required to locate them using the given formula.
Next, we loop through each item in the pantry and calculate the time required to locate it with every other item. We store these times in a 2D array called `$sorted_items`.
Finally, we sort the items in `$sorted_items` based on increasing time and output them in that order.
When we run this code, the output will be:
```
Boxed pasta
Cereal
Canned soup
Jarred sauce
```
This means that if we organize our pantry items in this order, we will minimize the time required to locate any two items in the pantry.

