Here is the modified C# code that can handle any data type array while calculating the sum of all odd elements:
```csharp
public static T SumOfOddElements<T>(T[] arr)
{
    dynamic sum = default(T);
    for (int i = 0; i < arr.Length; i++)
    {
        if (arr[i] is int && Convert.ToInt32(arr[i]) % 2 != 0)
        {
            sum += arr[i];
        }
        else if (arr[i] is long && Convert.ToInt64(arr[i]) % 2 != 0)
        {
            sum += arr[i];
        }
        else if (arr[i] is float && Convert.ToSingle(arr[i]) % 2 != 0)
        {
            sum += arr[i];
        }
        else if (arr[i] is double && Convert.ToDouble(arr[i]) % 2 != 0)
        {
            sum += arr[i];
        }
        else if (arr[i] is decimal && Convert.ToDecimal(arr[i]) % 2 != 0)
        {
            sum += arr[i];
        }
    }
    return sum;
}
```
In this modified code, we have used C# generics to specify the data type of the array. The `T` in the method declaration indicates that the method can accept an array of any data type.
We have also used the `dynamic` keyword to declare the `sum` variable. This allows us to add any type of data to the sum variable without knowing the data type at compile time.
Inside the for loop, we have used the `is` keyword to check the data type of each element in the array. If the element is an odd number, we add it to the sum variable. 
Finally, we return the sum variable as the result of the method.

