Yes, I can help you with that. Here's a sample C# code that can automatically correct subject-verb agreement in a markdown-formatted sentence:
```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;
class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        string input = "The group of diligent students is determined to pursue their studies.";
        string pattern = @"\b(is|was|has)\b\s+(?<subject>\w+)\s+(?<verb>\w+ed|\w+s)\b";
        Match match = Regex.Match(input, pattern);
        if (match.Success)
        {
            string subject = match.Groups["subject"].Value;
            string verb = match.Groups["verb"].Value;
            if (verb.EndsWith("s") && !subject.EndsWith("s"))
            {
                verb = verb.Substring(0, verb.Length - 1);
            }
            else if (verb.EndsWith("ed") && subject.EndsWith("s"))
            {
                verb += "t";
            }
            input = Regex.Replace(input, pattern, $"{verb} {subject}");
        }
        Console.WriteLine(input);
    }
}
```
This code uses regular expressions to match sentences that contain a subject-verb agreement error. It then corrects the error and outputs the corrected sentence.
To use this code, you can replace the `input` variable with your own sentence. The code will then output the corrected sentence.
Note that this code only handles simple cases of subject-verb agreement errors. It may not work correctly for more complex sentences.

